registerRefData('nsw-cond-n-59',{"id":59,"pan":null,"ref":"DA/2024/615/1","address":"173 Rickard Road Leppington 2179,183 Rickard Road Leppington 2179","description":"Commercial mixed use development comprising a retail shopping centre and hotel","outcome":"refused","outcome_raw":"Refused","outcome_source":"planning_panels","determined":"2026-05-06","notice_url":"https://apps.planningportal.nsw.gov.au/prweb/PRRestService/DocMgmt/v1/PublicDocuments/DATA-WORKATTACH-FILE PEC-DPE-EP-WORK PPSSWC-516!20260401T005350.624 GMT","sha256":"1963162f3562a5ce46799c1e0415e3f709c3cf2dd1c80516bc48b9520fdf33f0","pages":30,"layout":"numbered","n":148,"fetched_at":"2026-08-16T23:19:06+00:00","file":"n/59.js","source_page":"https://www.planningportal.nsw.gov.au/planning-panel/commercial-mixed-use-development-comprising-retail-shopping-centre-and-hotel","lga":"CAMDEN","conditions":[{"label":"1","code":null,"number":"1","title":"These Class 1 proceedings arise following the Council’s deemed refusal of the","text":"applicant’s development application no DA/2024/615/1 (DA) for a commercial mixed-\nuse development on rural land at 173 and 183 Rickard Road, Leppington (the Site).\nThe development is advanced as a strategic opportunity to activate the Leppington\nTown Centre and deliver a new civic precinct and retail attraction for Leppington.","stage":"unassigned","stage_basis":"unassigned","page":3},{"label":"2","code":null,"number":"2","title":"","text":"The Site is located immediately to the south of the Leppington Railway Station and\nTransport for New South Wales (TNSW) is the second respondent in the proceedings.","stage":"unassigned","stage_basis":"unassigned","page":3},{"label":"3","code":null,"number":"3","title":"","text":"The plans and documents in Ex C now form the development application for which\ndevelopment consent is sought, including the following single expert’s reports:\n(1) Individual expert report on bushfire issues filed 16 September 2025, authored by\nCorey Shackleton (Ex J);\n(2) Individual expert report on structural issues filed 17 September 2025, authored\nby Jow Salhani (Ex K);\n(3) Individual expert report on ESD and energy issues filed 17 September 2025,\nauthored by Neihad Al-Khalidy (Ex L);\n(4) Individual expert report on traffic issues filed 17 July 2025, authored by Robert\nVarga (Ex H); and\n(5) Individual expert report on arboricultural issues filed 18 September 2025,\nauthored by Alex de Jon (Ex M).","stage":"unassigned","stage_basis":"unassigned","page":3},{"label":"4","code":null,"number":"4","title":"","text":"In summary, the Proposed Development comprises:\n(1) subdivision of the Site into four lots;\n(2) construction of a four-storey retail shopping centre on the western lot;\n(3) construction of a six-storey hotel on the eastern lot;\n(4) basement parking;\n(5) road works; and\n(6) associated works.","stage":"unassigned","stage_basis":"unassigned","page":3},{"label":"5","code":null,"number":"5","title":"","text":"The layout of the Proposed Development can be understood from the Site plan below:\nOverview","stage":"unassigned","stage_basis":"unassigned","page":3},{"label":"6","code":null,"number":"6","title":"","text":"There are two remaining contentions between the Council and the applicant:\n(1) whether adequate arrangements have been made for public utilities\ninfrastructure for the purpose of cl 6.1(1) of Appendix 5 of State Environmental\nPlanning Policy (Precincts-Western Parkland City) 2021 (SEPP WPC)\n(contention 2); and\n(2) whether, although the development is fully compliant with the current Precinct\nPlan (Camden Growth Centres Precinct Plan 2013) under the SEPP WPC,\ndetermining weight should be given to the Leppington Town Centre Planning\nProposal 2023 (LTCPP) under s 4.15 (1)(a)(ii) of the Environmental Planning\nand Assessment Act 1979 (NSW) (EPA Act), resulting in the refusal of consent\n(contentions 3 to 5).","stage":"unassigned","stage_basis":"unassigned","page":4},{"label":"7","code":null,"number":"7","title":"","text":"The Site falls within the Leppington North Precinct of the Camden Growth Centres\nPrecinct Plan 2013 (the Precinct Plan). Because the development has an estimated\ndevelopment cost of over $30 million, this application must be assessed against the\ncontrols set out in the Precinct Plan at Appendix 5 of the SEPP WPC, and the Camden\nGrowth Centre Precincts Development Control Plan 2025 (current DCP), including the\nprecinct specific controls in Schedule 2 - Leppington Major Centre.","stage":"unassigned","stage_basis":"unassigned","page":4},{"label":"8","code":null,"number":"8","title":"","text":"The land use table under the Precinct Plan identifies that most of the Site is within Zone\nB3 - Commercial Core, with a relatively small area of the Site adjacent to Rickards\nRoad zoned SP2 - Infrastructure for future road widening purposes. Therefore, the\nProposed Development is permissible with consent.","stage":"unassigned","stage_basis":"unassigned","page":5},{"label":"9","code":null,"number":"9","title":"","text":"However, for the development to be serviced, there needs to be an extension of the\nreticulation sewer and water main to the Site.","stage":"unassigned","stage_basis":"unassigned","page":5},{"label":"10","code":null,"number":"10","title":"","text":"The evidence is that the Proposed Development requires the construction of a sewer\nline through at least the property located at 134 Dickson Road to the west of the Site\n(Ex C, Tab 13).","stage":"unassigned","stage_basis":"unassigned","page":5},{"label":"11","code":null,"number":"11","title":"","text":"Relevantly, cl 6.1 of Appendix 5 of SEPP WPC, requires the Court, exercising the\npower of the consent authority, to be satisfied (prior to the grant of consent) that\n“adequate arrangements have been made” to make the sewer and water infrastructure\navailable when required: Georges River Council v Eskander [2024] NSWLEC 98\n(Eskander) [54]-[56]. That is, “adequate arrangements” must already exist and be\nsupported by some form of tangible and objective proof before the grant of\ndevelopment consent: Eskander [57].","stage":"unassigned","stage_basis":"unassigned","page":5},{"label":"12","code":null,"number":"12","title":"","text":"The Council raises no issue with the application that relates to cl 6.1 and the water\ninfrastructure which will be on land controlled by the Council. However, the Council\ndoes take issue with the construction of a sewer line through land including 134\nDickson Road to the west of the Site, being land that is not owned by ALAND (the\nowner of the Site) (Ex C Tab 18). Put simply, the Council argues in the absence of\n“tangible and objective evidence” of any adequate arrangement with the adjoining\nowner for construction of the sewer works on its land that the Court cannot form the\npositive state of satisfaction mandated by cl 6 (CWS at [12]-[16]).","stage":"unassigned","stage_basis":"unassigned","page":5},{"label":"13","code":null,"number":"13","title":"","text":"The applicant contends that it has made adequate arrangements with Sydney Water to\nensure the disposal and management of sewer works from the Proposed Development,\nwhich will be made available when required. It relies upon the powers of Sydney Water\nto enter the Site to carry out the works; or by way of a s 4.6 request to make good its\ncase (AWS at [35]- [43]).","stage":"unassigned","stage_basis":"unassigned","page":5},{"label":"14","code":null,"number":"14","title":"","text":"As the cl 6.1 contention raises a jurisdictional precondition to the grant of consent it\nneeds to be dealt with before any consideration of the merits.","stage":"unassigned","stage_basis":"unassigned","page":5},{"label":"15","code":null,"number":"15","title":"The second contention concerns the Council’s exhibited Leppington Town Centre","text":"Planning Proposal (Ex C) (Planning Proposal).","stage":"unassigned","stage_basis":"unassigned","page":5},{"label":"16","code":null,"number":"16","title":"This Planning Proposal was submitted by the Council, in conjunction with Liverpool","text":"Council, in 2023. In early December 2024, and after the Council’s public consultation\nconcluded, the State, through the Department of Planning (the Department), took\nownership as a State-Assessed Rezoning Proposal (SARP). This was done pursuant to\nthe Department of Planning, Housing and Infrastructure’s State Significant Rezoning\nPolicy (2024) (the Policy) (Ex 1R2, Tab 19, 1259).","stage":"unassigned","stage_basis":"unassigned","page":5},{"label":"17","code":null,"number":"17","title":"","text":"As was explained at the hearing, the Policy provides a pathway for sites to be\nidentified, and their rezoning “fast tracked to deliver strategically important land for new\nhousing and economic opportunities” (Ex 1R2, 1262). The timing of that fast-tracked\nprocess in a SARP is 180 to 205 working days from the initial consideration of the\nproposal through to finalisation (Ex 1R2, 1264). Following exhibition, there is then a 40-\nday period of post exhibition amendments, after which the planning proposal proceeds\nto “finalisation” (Ex 1R2, 1624). As part of the finalisation process stage “the\nDepartment [is] responsible for the preparation of all necessary environmental planning\ninstruments and maps, engaging with the NSW Parliamentary Counsel’s office and\nPublic Spaces approval” (Ex 1R2, 172).","stage":"unassigned","stage_basis":"unassigned","page":6},{"label":"18","code":null,"number":"18","title":"","text":"The evidence is that the Department is presently engaged in the finalisation process of\npost exhibition amendments (Ex 1R5 at [265]). And, while the Policy suggests that the\nproponent of the Indicative Layout Plan (ILP) will be given an opportunity to update its\nrezoning proposal, any technical studies and any Site specific DCP (Ex 1R5,1272), it is\nacknowledged that the Department is not bound by the terms of the Policy. Particularly,\nin circumstances where, as in this case, the Policy does not contemplate the situation\nwhere a rezoning is called in by the Department after exhibition and significant work\nhas already been undertaken by the proponent.","stage":"unassigned","stage_basis":"unassigned","page":6},{"label":"19","code":null,"number":"19","title":"","text":"On 26 August 2024 the Senior Legal Officer at the Department wrote to Council’s\nsolicitors stating:\n“…the Department is currently considering post-exhibition changes as part of the SARPfinalisation process. At this time, these post exhibition changes continue to identifyretention of a publicly accessible plaza on the [Site], though the final form and size havenot been finalised. Any recommended post-exhibition changes will be subject to finalapproval through the SARP finalisation process. The SARP is anticipated to be finalisedby 30 March 2026”. (Ex 1R5 at [265] Appendix P); (T270:22-25).","stage":"unassigned","stage_basis":"unassigned","page":6},{"label":"20","code":null,"number":"20","title":"","text":"On 13 August 2025, the Department emailed the applicant and provided a slideshow\nshowing a more recent iteration of the Department’s plans for the Site than those\nshown in the draft ILP (Ex 1R2 Tab 14, Ex IR3 Tab 7). The applicant submits that the\ndifferences between the two ILPs are “stark” demonstrating that the terms of the\ninstruments are uncertain. It contends the projected finalisation date of 30 March 2026\nis not a fixed deadline and at best the Department’s best estimate (AWS at [68-69]).","stage":"unassigned","stage_basis":"unassigned","page":6},{"label":"21","code":null,"number":"21","title":"Notwithstanding some changes (CWS at [51]), the Council maintains its position that","text":"the draft Planning Proposal is both certain in its terms and imminent in its making and\ntherefore must be given significant weight in my assessment of the application.","stage":"unassigned","stage_basis":"unassigned","page":6},{"label":"22","code":null,"number":"22","title":"","text":"The Council emphasises that the Site is located within the inner core of the proposed\ntown centre under the Planning Proposal (Ex 1R2, 741). And, it submits that a simple\noverlay of the initially proposed zoning map with the Site plan (green being the park,\nand the darker diagonal lines being the east-west linear plaza) demonstrates that the\nDA and draft ILP cannot be reconciled. If approved, the Council contends that the\ndevelopment will preclude the planned public (civic) park in the centre of the Site, open\nspace plazas (north-south and linear east-west) and undermine the proposed \"density\npyramid\" surrounding the train station and the intended construction of a 25m wide\neast-west road (T141:40-50; T142:1-5; T143:24-30) (Ex 1R, 741; Ex 1R2, 970).","stage":"unassigned","stage_basis":"unassigned","page":6},{"label":"23","code":null,"number":"23","title":"","text":"The contested issues and other matters were addressed in the following Joint Expert\nreports:\nApplicant ExpertCouncil ExpertTNSW\nExpert\nJoint Civil, Stormwater and\nGroundwater Expert Report filed\n30 September 2025 (Ex 1R4)\nJoe Bacha (Civil)\nLee Douglas (Land\nand Water,\nGroundwater\nStudies)\nRitesh Shrestha\n(Civil)\nBrendan Tong\n(Civil)\nJoint Town planning and Urban\nDesign Expert Report filed 30\nSeptember 2025 (Ex 1R5)\nSimon Wilkes\n(Town Planner)\nPeter Smith (Urban\nDesigner)\nRyan Pritchard\n(Town Planner -\nStatutory\nPlanning)\nGraham Matthews\n(Town Planner -\nStrategic\nPlanning)\nGianluca (Luca)\nDragone (Urban\nDesigner)\nJoint Aboriginal Heritage Expert\nReport filed 3 October 2025 (Ex\n1R6)\nDr Penny McCardle\n(Aboriginal\nHeritage)\nDr Samantha\nHiggs (Aboriginal\nHeritage)\nJoint Financial Feasibility Expert\nReport filed 10 October 2025\n(Ex 1R7)\nGlenn Lamont\n(Financial\nFeasibility)\nAdrian Hack\n(Financial\nFeasibility)\nSupplementary Joint Aboriginal\nHeritage Expert Report filed 10\nOctober 2025 (Ex 1R8)\nDr Penny McCardle\n(Aboriginal\nHeritage)\nDr Samantha\nHiggs (Aboriginal\nHeritage)\nJoint Traffic Expert Report filed\n10 October 2025 (Ex 1R9)\nRobert Varga\n(Traffic and\nParking)\nRitesh Shrestha\n(Traffic and\nParking)\nKen\nHollyoak\n(Traffic and\nParking)\nSupplementary Joint Expert\nReport - Town Planning and\nUrban Design Issues dated 15\nOctober 2025 (Ex 1R10)\nSimon Wilkes\n(Town Planner)\nPeter Smith (Urban\nDesigner)\nRyan Pritchard\n(Town Planner -\nStatutory\nPlanning)\nGraham Matthews\n(Town Planner -\nStrategic\nPlanning)\nGianluca (Luca)\nDragone (Urban\nDesigner)","stage":"unassigned","stage_basis":"unassigned","page":7},{"label":"24","code":null,"number":"24","title":"","text":"As a result of the applicant’s single experts’ reports, joint reporting and the preparation\nof further amended plans and documents, all of TNSW’s contentions have been\naddressed to their satisfaction. Having been assisted by the applicant’s and Council’s\ndetailed written submissions and hearing transcripts, I now deal with the two remaining\ncontentions.\nContention 2 - cl 6.1 adequate arrangements","stage":"unassigned","stage_basis":"unassigned","page":8},{"label":"25","code":null,"number":"25","title":"","text":"Clause 6.1 provides that:\n(1) The consent authority must not grant development consent to development on landto which this Precinct Plan applies unless it is satisfied that any public utilityinfrastructure that is essential for the proposed development is available or thatadequate arrangements have been made to make that infrastructure available whenrequired.\n(2) In this section, public utility infrastructure includes infrastructure for any of thefollowing-\n(a) the supply of water,\n(b) the supply of electricity,\n(c) the disposal and management of sewage.\n(3) This section does not apply to development for the purpose of providing, extending,augmenting, maintaining or repairing any public utility infrastructure referred to in thissection.","stage":"unassigned","stage_basis":"unassigned","page":9},{"label":"26","code":null,"number":"26","title":"","text":"Both parties rely on the decision of Eskander to make good their case. In considering\nthe meaning of the requirement that “adequate arrangements have been made” in\nrespect of services essential for a development, Robson J said at [56]-[57]:\n“56 I find that the state of satisfaction thereby requires consideration at a differentmoment in time, such that the adequate arrangements must have “been made”. That is,there must be a current “arrangement” that has “been” made. That “arrangement” is thatthe “essential” service (here, suitable vehicular access) be “available when required”,that is, in the future. Put another way, the service that is not presently available, will beavailable, and it will be available because adequate arrangements have “been made” tomake it available.\n57 Further, while it is clear that something less than a formal or binding promise isrequired, I find that there must be some form of objective and tangible proof toconstitute an arrangement as something “made”. It is in this context that I accept thewords quoted by Stein JA in Codlea at 378, to the effect that such an arrangement mustbe “at least, a willingness on the part of the relevant authorities to co-operate in aconsensual way …”. However, as Stein JA also found, proof of such an arrangementmust “exist” and must “pre-exist the ‘satisfaction’ of [the] council with [it]”. It is in thissense that I consider the comments of Preston CJ of LEC in Crighton Properties, that“arrangement”, within the context of an environmental planning instrument, means“something in the nature of an understanding between two or more persons” (at [42]),should be seen. Again, considered in this way, the fact that the arrangement is tangible,gives a consent authority the ability then to determine if the qualities or characteristicsof an arrangement that has been made for the supply of (in this case, suitable vehicularaccess) a service in the future, is “adequate”.”\nApplicant’s position","stage":"unassigned","stage_basis":"unassigned","page":9},{"label":"27","code":null,"number":"27","title":"","text":"In submitting “that adequate arrangements have been made to make the disposal and\nmanagement of sewerage available when required”, the applicant refers to\narrangements between the site landowner ALAND with Sydney Water (AWS at [33]).","stage":"unassigned","stage_basis":"unassigned","page":9},{"label":"28","code":null,"number":"28","title":"","text":"The applicant submits at the relevant time, the date of the hearing, the Court has before\nit “objective and tangible proof that an arrangement has been made” for sewer\ninfrastructure to be available for the development when required by cl 6.1\n(Eskander [23]).","stage":"unassigned","stage_basis":"unassigned","page":9},{"label":"29","code":null,"number":"29","title":"","text":"The “objective and tangible proof” relied upon by the applicant, for the purposes of cl\n6.1(1), is the letter prepared by Hill Consulting Engineers Pty Ltd dated 15 September\n2025 and other documents in Ex C, Tab 13 which are said to be evidence that:\n(1) The applicant has made an application under s 73 of the Sydney Water Act\n1994 (NSW) (SW Act) to obtain a compliance certificate in relation to the supply\nof water and disposal of sewerage at the Site (Ex C, Tab 13, p 201);\n(2) Sydney Water has issued its Notice of Requirements following the s 73\napplication (Ex C, Tab 21); and\n(3) A Developer Works Deed has been entered into with Sydney Water on 11\nSeptember 2025 (DWD) (Ex C, Tab 13, p 234).","stage":"unassigned","stage_basis":"unassigned","page":10},{"label":"30","code":null,"number":"30","title":"","text":"The applicant submits that the DWD is an “arrangement” and a contract that is more\nthan “something in the nature of an understanding” between ALAND and Sydney Water\nthat is evidence that the necessary sewer infrastructure for the proposed development\nwill be made available when required.","stage":"unassigned","stage_basis":"unassigned","page":10},{"label":"31","code":null,"number":"31","title":"","text":"The applicant submits that the letter of 15 September 2025 from Milos Salatic, (Senior\nWater Servicing Coordinator for Sydney Water) is evidence that the plans referred to\ntherein are consistent with and maintain compliance with the Sydney Water Notice of\nRequirements (Ex C, Tab 19).","stage":"unassigned","stage_basis":"unassigned","page":10},{"label":"32","code":null,"number":"32","title":"","text":"The applicant emphasises Mr Salatic’s explanation in Part 4 of his letter that the sewer\nline extension for this development does not require an easement: s 1.3 of Sydney\nWater’s Easements Operational Guidelines (2022).","stage":"unassigned","stage_basis":"unassigned","page":10},{"label":"33","code":null,"number":"33","title":"","text":"Furthermore, that Sydney Waster has power under s 37(2) of the SW Act to:\n…operate, repair, replace, maintain, remove, extend, expand, connect, disconnect,improve or do any other things that are necessary or appropriate to any of its works toensure that, in the opinion of the Corporation, the works are used in an efficient matterfor the purposes for which they were installed.","stage":"unassigned","stage_basis":"unassigned","page":10},{"label":"34","code":null,"number":"34","title":"","text":"Accepting that an easement is not required by Sydney Water, the applicant submits that\nthere is now no need for landowner’s consent for the installation of the sewer\ninfrastructure on the adjoining land or to allow Sydney Water to enter the adjoining\nland. It contends that policies entitled “Entry onto third party property” and “Entry onto\nthird party property - Water Servicing Coordinators (WSCs)/Developers” (Ex 1R3, Tabs\n2 and 3) authorise Sydney Water to access neighbouring land when required to\nconstruct necessary infrastructure, subject to the notice requirements in s 40(1).","stage":"unassigned","stage_basis":"unassigned","page":10},{"label":"35","code":null,"number":"35","title":"","text":"The applicant submits that Condition 5 of Part 1.1 of the draft conditions of consent also\nensures that the sewer infrastructure will be constructed as part of the development\nconsent in the event of an approval. The condition states:\n“For the avoidance of doubt:\n(a) - (d)…\n(e) The approved development does not include the construction of water and sewerreticulation systems that are not within the site. However, it is a requirement of thisconsent that the applicant must obtain:\nConfirmation that Sydney Water is satisfied with its building plans (including theplans for the external works), prior to the issue of any Subdivision WorksCertificate;\nAny required development consent for the necessary off-site water or sewerworks reticulation systems, prior to the issue of a Subdivision Works Certificate;and\nThe issue of a compliance certificate under Section 73 of the Sydney Water Act1994 prior to the issue of any Occupation Certificate or Subdivision Certificate.”","stage":"unassigned","stage_basis":"unassigned","page":10},{"label":"36","code":null,"number":"36","title":"The applicant emphasises that the condition foreshadows further approvals including","text":"any requirement for a development application for the necessary off-site works or\nsewer works, prior to the issue of any Subdivision Works Certificate, but does not\nexcuse the need for assessment of any off-site works now. In that regard, the applicant\nsubmits that the potential impacts of the proposed sewer works have been assessed\nand that the Court has sufficient evidence to approve the Proposed Development\nsubject to the Grampian condition. The applicant relies on the arboricultural (Ex B, Tab\n44), ecological (Ex B, Tab 45) and Aboriginal heritage (Ex C, Tab 20) reports filed in the\nproceedings and highlights that no allegation has been made that the assessment is in\nany way inadequate or that the potential impacts are unacceptable.\nCouncil’s position","stage":"unassigned","stage_basis":"unassigned","page":11},{"label":"37","code":null,"number":"37","title":"The Council contends that the applicant’s arrangements with Sydney Water do not","text":"reach the necessary threshold to satisfy cl 6.1. Furthermore, that its reliance on a cl 4.6\nrequest proceeds from the incorrect premise that cl 6.1 is a development standard.\nRecourse to Sydney Water’s powers","stage":"unassigned","stage_basis":"unassigned","page":11},{"label":"38","code":null,"number":"38","title":"","text":"The Council acknowledges that Sydney Water has the power to enter a site to\nconstruct new works: s 38(1)(a) of the SW Act. And, that under s 39 of the SW Act,\nauthorised officers can issue certificates of authority to other persons to exercise the\npowers of entry. However, the Council emphasises, as the letter from the Water\nServices Coordinator states, that “… Sydney Water is prepared to use these powers\nwhen a landowner has unreasonably denied access” (Emphasis added) (Ex 1R5,\n100).","stage":"unassigned","stage_basis":"unassigned","page":11},{"label":"39","code":null,"number":"39","title":"","text":"Further, the Council submits there is no evidence before the Court that the adjoining\nlandowner has unreasonably denied access in this case. In fact, the Council submits,\nthere is no evidence of:\n(1) Any approaches to the adjoining landowner for permission to carry out the\nworks;\n(2) Any assessment of how intrusive those works will be for that landowner; and\n(3) Whether, in the circumstances, Sydney Water would authorise the applicant to\nexercise Sydney Water’s powers of entry in s 38.","stage":"unassigned","stage_basis":"unassigned","page":12},{"label":"40","code":null,"number":"40","title":"","text":"The Council also emphasises that Sydney Water will not be carrying out the sewer\nworks, the applicant will be required to carry out the works under the consent (Ex 1R5,\n99; draft Condition 5 of the Proposed Conditions of Consent).","stage":"unassigned","stage_basis":"unassigned","page":12},{"label":"41","code":null,"number":"41","title":"Therefore, there is no tangible or objective evidence that adequate arrangements have","text":"been made between the applicant and the adjoining land owner to make the sewer\ninfrastructure available when required. The Council submits that the letter from the\nWater Services Coordinator is inadequate and the terms of the DWD (largely contained\nwithin Schedule 1) at 3.1 place the responsibility upon the applicant to obtain entry and\naccess rights to the land it needs for the works (Ex C, Tab 13, 238).","stage":"unassigned","stage_basis":"unassigned","page":12},{"label":"42","code":null,"number":"42","title":"","text":"That clause states relevantly (Ex C, Tab 13):\n“(a) The Developer is responsible for obtaining any entry and access rights to OtherLand that it needs for the Developer Works.\n(b)The Developer must not start the Developer Works until it has the written consentof the Other Land’s owner and any tenants.\n…\n(f) If the Developer requires access to enter Other Land, the Developer must obtain thewritten permission of the relevant property owners and tenants, and:\ni. The Developer must use Sydney Water’s Permission to Enter form toevidence consent, which is available on Sydney Water’s website or can beobtained from the WSC.\nii. The Developer must comply with the requirements set out in Sydney Water’sPermission to Enter form, including payment of compensation\niii. The Developer is responsible for all costs of delay and mediation involved inresolving any disputes.”\n(Emphasis added)","stage":"unassigned","stage_basis":"unassigned","page":12},{"label":"43","code":null,"number":"43","title":"","text":"Other land is defined as “Land owned or occupied by a Person other than the\nDeveloper or Sydney Water, which the Developer and the Accredited Providers need\nentry and access to” (Ex C, Tab 13).","stage":"unassigned","stage_basis":"unassigned","page":12},{"label":"44","code":null,"number":"44","title":"","text":"The Council contends that none of those provisions indicate that Sydney Water is\ninclined to exercise its powers to authorise the applicant to enter the adjoining land to\ninstall the infrastructure. Rather, they suggest the opposite; that the applicant must\nobtain the consent of the landowner.","stage":"unassigned","stage_basis":"unassigned","page":12},{"label":"45","code":null,"number":"45","title":"","text":"The Council accepts that to be an ‘arrangement’, it is not necessary that there be a\nbinding agreement (Codlea Pty Ltd v Byron Shire Council (1999) 105 LGERA 370;\n[1999] NSWCA 399 (Codlea), [39]-[40]), and it is sufficient that there be an\nunderstanding arrived at between two or more persons: Eskander [57]. However, it\nsubmits that in this case, there is simply no evidence of any understanding being\nreached, and currently in place, between the applicant and the landowner of 134\nDickson Road to enable the Court to form the requisite state of satisfaction under cl 6.1.\nNor is there evidence of any understanding between the applicant and Sydney Water\nthat Sydney Water will exercise its powers to enable the works to proceed (absent\nlandowner’s consent).","stage":"unassigned","stage_basis":"unassigned","page":13},{"label":"46","code":null,"number":"46","title":"","text":"The Council contends that without knowing the position of the landowner of 134\nDickson Road, there remains a realistic possibility that Sydney Water will not exercise\nits powers and that there is insufficient evidence before the Court to satisfy the\nprovision: Eskander [60]-[62].\nFinding - cl 6.1","stage":"unassigned","stage_basis":"unassigned","page":13},{"label":"47","code":null,"number":"47","title":"Clause 6.1 is a jurisdictional provision which prohibits the grant of development","text":"consent, unless I form a particular state of satisfaction.","stage":"unassigned","stage_basis":"unassigned","page":13},{"label":"48","code":null,"number":"48","title":"","text":"The subject matter of cl 6.1 concerns, relevantly in this case, that adequate\narrangements have been made to make sewer infrastructure available when required\nbecause the sewer pipe extending to the west of the Site is not available despite it\nbeing essential for the servicing of the Proposed Development.","stage":"unassigned","stage_basis":"unassigned","page":13},{"label":"49","code":null,"number":"49","title":"The clause refers to “arrangements have been made” thereby identifying a temporal","text":"point for the state of positive satisfaction (Eskander [56]). They cannot be contemplated\nin the future; the nature of the arrangement is current and must be prior to the grant of\ndevelopment consent (Eskander [57]).","stage":"unassigned","stage_basis":"unassigned","page":13},{"label":"50","code":null,"number":"50","title":"The “adequate arrangements” are also not expressly limited to adequate arrangements","text":"with Sydney Water, as the applicant would have it. To my thinking the clause also\ninvites a consideration, in the circumstances of this case, of the position of the adjoining\nlandowner at 134 Dickson Road, on whose land the work is required to be undertaken.","stage":"unassigned","stage_basis":"unassigned","page":13},{"label":"51","code":null,"number":"51","title":"","text":"As the applicant submits the nature of the arrangement depends upon the context of\nthe particular provision. In Codlea it was accepted that evidence of a willingness on the\npart of the relevant authorities to co-operate in a consensual way effected the\narrangement. In Crighton Properties Pty Ltd v Kiama Municipal Council (2006) 146\nLGERA 271; [2006] NSWLEC 297, Preston CJ observed that arrangement means\nsomething in the nature of an understanding, and in Eskander the Court held that there\n“must be some form of objective and tangible proof to constitute an arrangement as\nsomething made’” (Eskander [57]).","stage":"unassigned","stage_basis":"unassigned","page":13},{"label":"52","code":null,"number":"52","title":"","text":"The applicant argues that the letter from Hill Consulting Engineers (Ex C Tab 13)\nwritten by Mr Milos to Mr Wilkes (the applicant’s consultant) is relevant as it evidences\nthat Sydney Water does not require an easement for assets of this kind (Heading 4, p\n202). However, as the Council points out, that is not the end of the inquiry because\nmore than access is required to provide the sewer infrastructure. The applicant does\nnot own the land where the sewer infrastructure is proposed. The documents the\napplicant relies upon refer to the consent of the relevant landowner(s) for the\ninfrastructure to be actually installed.","stage":"unassigned","stage_basis":"unassigned","page":14},{"label":"53","code":null,"number":"53","title":"","text":"Under the Heading 5 options, Mr Milos states that if the adjoining landowner’s consent\nis withheld (described as an unlikely event), Sydney Water has powers of entry under s\n38 of the SW Act. And, in that event Sydney Water is prepared to use these powers of\nentry when a landowner has unreasonably denied access.","stage":"unassigned","stage_basis":"unassigned","page":14},{"label":"54","code":null,"number":"54","title":"","text":"As the Council submits, the premise for the proposition that Sydney Water is prepared\nto use its powers to enter does not arise on the evidence. I have no understanding of\nwhether there has been an unreasonable denial of access by the adjoining landowner.\nIn fact, there is no evidence as to any communication with the adjoining landowner.","stage":"unassigned","stage_basis":"unassigned","page":14},{"label":"55","code":null,"number":"55","title":"","text":"The feasibility letter from Sydney Water (also relied upon by the applicant to support its\nadequate arrangements) under the heading “5.2 Entry onto the neighbour’s property”\nrefers to forms provided by a listed water provider (WSC) that must be signed by the\nowner of the land to which entry is sought (Ex P Tab 4). The letter also contemplates, if\nrequired, the WSC negotiating with the adjoining landowner for permission to enter and\nthe payment of compensation. I have no evidence of these matters being addressed.","stage":"unassigned","stage_basis":"unassigned","page":14},{"label":"56","code":null,"number":"56","title":"","text":"The DWD (Ex P Tab 5) also requires the applicant to obtain entry and access rights\nfrom the adjoining owner, prior to the carrying out of the infrastructure works. Again,\nthere is no evidence that the requirement has been satisfied or permission sought from\nthe adjoining landowner to satisfy the terms of the DWD.","stage":"unassigned","stage_basis":"unassigned","page":14},{"label":"57","code":null,"number":"57","title":"","text":"At this critical stage, all I know is that Sydney Water does not require an easement for\naccess and that s 3.2 concerning easements is not engaged. However, this does not\nrestrict the operation of cl A3.1 and there must be some property right that is engaged\nby the developer in order to carry out these sewer works and permanently install a pipe\non its neighbour’s land (Ex C; T322:1-12).","stage":"unassigned","stage_basis":"unassigned","page":14},{"label":"58","code":null,"number":"58","title":"","text":"At the time of this decision, contrary to the applicant’s submissions, there is no\nsatisfactory evidence before the Court addressing the impacts of the installation of the\nproposed sewer works on the adjoining land. No understanding of how intrusive they\nwill be or the level of detriment, if any, to the adjoining landowner’s property. I am not\nprepared to impose the applicant’s Condition 5 Part 1.1. The deferral of these essential\nworks is inappropriate. Clause 6.1 mandates a state of satisfaction prior to the grant of\nconsent (Eskander [21]).","stage":"unassigned","stage_basis":"unassigned","page":14},{"label":"59","code":null,"number":"59","title":"","text":"Accordingly, in the absence of satisfactory evidence I cannot form the requisite state of\nsatisfaction under cl 6.1 and the clause requires me to refuse this application.\nAlternative arrangement - clause 4.6 request","stage":"unassigned","stage_basis":"unassigned","page":15},{"label":"60","code":null,"number":"60","title":"","text":"I have read the parties’ competing submissions in respect of this issue.","stage":"unassigned","stage_basis":"unassigned","page":15},{"label":"61","code":null,"number":"61","title":"","text":"The applicant relies on a written cl 4.6 request for relaxation of cl 6.1 (Ex 1R5\nAppendix).","stage":"unassigned","stage_basis":"unassigned","page":15},{"label":"62","code":null,"number":"62","title":"","text":"As the Council submits for the applicant’s argument to be accepted, not only must cl\n6.1 constitute a development standard but the applicant must establish that the\nProposed Development will contravene a development standard.","stage":"unassigned","stage_basis":"unassigned","page":15},{"label":"63","code":null,"number":"63","title":"","text":"As the construction of the sewer pipe is not part of the application, cl 4.6 cannot be\nemployed to vary cl 6.1 as it is not a variation of the Proposed Development. I accept\nthe Council’s submission that the argument fails at the first hurdle.","stage":"unassigned","stage_basis":"unassigned","page":15},{"label":"64","code":null,"number":"64","title":"","text":"As to what is a development standard, I start with the definition in s 1.4 of the EPA Act.\nIt states:\n… provisions of an environmental planning instrument or the regulations in relation tothe carrying out of development, being provisions by or under which requirements arespecified or standards are fixed in respect of any aspect of that development…","stage":"unassigned","stage_basis":"unassigned","page":15},{"label":"65","code":null,"number":"65","title":"","text":"For a provision to fall within the definition of a development standard, “there must\nbe a development in respect of an aspect of which the provision specifies a\nrequirement or fixes a standard”: Strathfield Municipal Council v Poynting (2001) 116\nLGERA 319; [2001] NSWCA 270 at [96].","stage":"unassigned","stage_basis":"unassigned","page":15},{"label":"66","code":null,"number":"66","title":"","text":"Clause 6.1 does not strictly ‘specify’ any requirement or standard. As the Council\nsubmits the usual meaning of “specify” is to:\n(1) “indicate with unambiguous clarity” (Deputy Federal Commissioner of Taxation v\nGillis (2003) 59 NSWLR 153; [2003] NSWCA 340 at [27]);\n(2) state “in explicit terms” and with “clarity and precision” (Tickner v Chapman\n(1995) 89 LGERA 1; (1995) 57 FCR 451 (Tickner) at 457, 480-481 and 491;\nJolly v District Council of Yorketown (1968) 119 CLR 347; [1968] HCA 55 at\n351).","stage":"unassigned","stage_basis":"unassigned","page":15},{"label":"67","code":null,"number":"67","title":"","text":"The statutory concept is not satisfied by “vague generalities”: Tickner at 457.","stage":"unassigned","stage_basis":"unassigned","page":15},{"label":"68","code":null,"number":"68","title":"","text":"As the Council submits, cl 6.1 is expressed in general terms. It requires satisfaction that\nrelevant infrastructure is available or that adequate arrangements have been made.\nThat is a general requirement far from “specifying” a requirement, still less “fixing” a\n“standard”. The provision does not require specific infrastructure or infrastructure to a\nparticular standard. It does not prescribe or specify a requirement or standard relating\nto any aspect of the proposed development: Strathfield Municipal Council v Poynting\n(2001) 116 LGERA 319; [2001] NSWCA 270 at [96].","stage":"unassigned","stage_basis":"unassigned","page":15},{"label":"69","code":null,"number":"69","title":"","text":"The facts of this case can be distinguished from the decision of the Court in Canterbury\nBankstown Council v Dib [2022] NSWLEC 79 (Dib), and I do not agree with the letter of\nadvice relied upon by the applicant (Ex C Tab 13). As the Court held in Dib at [66]:\n“…the test of whether provisions of an environmental panning instrument aredevelopment standards or not is simply whether they are “provisions in relation to thecarrying out of development” and “provisions by or under which requirements arespecified, or standards are fixed in respect of any aspect of that development”.”","stage":"unassigned","stage_basis":"unassigned","page":16},{"label":"70","code":null,"number":"70","title":"I also reject the applicant’s comparison with the development standard interrogated in","text":"the decision in Residents Against Improper Development Incorporated & Anor v Chase\nProperty Investments Pty Ltd (2006) 149 LGERA 360; [2006] NSWCA 323 (T323:48-\n50). In that case the Court was dealing with a standard that prescribed disposal of\nsewage in a particular manner. In this case there is a requirement for adequate\narrangements to be made; there is no prescribed method of disposal specified as an\naspect of the development in cl 6.1.","stage":"unassigned","stage_basis":"unassigned","page":16},{"label":"71","code":null,"number":"71","title":"","text":"In any event, if I were to accept that cl 6.1 was a development standard capable of\nvariation under cl 4.6 and that the sewer works were part of the Proposed\nDevelopment, I accept the Council’s criticisms of the applicant’s request. In particular,\nthe request fails to identify any environment planning grounds to justify the variation\nsought. Instead, it heralds the benefit of the development as a whole rather than\naddressing the element of the development that contravenes the standard: Initial Action\nPty Ltd v Woollahra Municipal Council (2018) 236 LGERA 256; [2018] NSWLEC 118.\nAlthough the inadequacy of the request is understandable, as cl 4.6 has no application\nin this case.","stage":"unassigned","stage_basis":"unassigned","page":16},{"label":"72","code":null,"number":"72","title":"","text":"For these reasons I reject the applicant’s cl 4.6 request.","stage":"unassigned","stage_basis":"unassigned","page":16},{"label":"73","code":null,"number":"73","title":"","text":"On any view my decision on cl 6.1 is an end to the appeal. However, in the event that I\nam wrong in this finding I have dealt with the second contention in respect to the draft\ninstrument, as I accept that all other contentions have been resolved including the tree\nissue.\nContentions 3 to 5 - Planning Proposal","stage":"unassigned","stage_basis":"unassigned","page":16},{"label":"74","code":null,"number":"74","title":"At [44]-[69] of its written submissions, the Council identifies documents comprising of","text":"the Planning Proposal and “the best evidence before the Court of the departments’\nproposed changes to the Council’s draft precinct plan”. The applicant sets out at [53] of\nits written submissions “a package of documents, including a draft planning instrument,\na draft DCP and a Contributions plan and possibly other documents” which in its view\nconstitutes the Planning Proposal.","stage":"unassigned","stage_basis":"unassigned","page":16},{"label":"75","code":null,"number":"75","title":"While the applicant accepts that the Planning Proposal is a relevant consideration","text":"under s 4.15(1)(a)(ii) of the EPA Act, it contends that the Council has failed to establish\nthat the Planning Proposal is both certain and imminent. Therefore, it contends that the\nPlanning Proposal should be given minimal weight and the fully compliant application\nunder the current controls be approved.","stage":"unassigned","stage_basis":"unassigned","page":16},{"label":"76","code":null,"number":"76","title":"","text":"The applicant asserts that at least three officers of the Department with relevant\nknowledge should have been called by the Council to assist the Court with an update\non the timing of the Planning Proposal’s finalisation. In the absence of such evidence\nthe Court is unable to draw an inference that the Department would not have assisted\nthe Council’s case.","stage":"unassigned","stage_basis":"unassigned","page":17},{"label":"77","code":null,"number":"77","title":"The applicant argues that the Planning Proposal has undergone significant change","text":"since being taken over by the Department. It contends that the proposed rezoning of\nthe Site from RE1 to B4 and the change to the public space through an unexplained\nDCP process supports its argument that the draft instrument is far from settled\n(T161:47; T162:21). (It rejects Mr Matthew’s evidence that a process of acquisition\nthrough a DCP (similar to that followed by Canada Bay Council) is appropriate and\ncontends that his evidence only adds to the uncertainty of the draft instrument.)","stage":"unassigned","stage_basis":"unassigned","page":17},{"label":"78","code":null,"number":"78","title":"","text":"The proposed changes to the maximum height of buildings control, the FSR control for\nthe Site (and possibility other sites) (Ex 1R3 p 24-25), the changes to the bus terminal\n(which TNSW oppose) (Ex R4), and the alteration of the commuter car park on the\nsouth side of the station under the LTCPP foreshadowed on 10 October are all\nsubmitted as indicative of an unsettled draft Planning Proposal (T205:24). And, the\nprospect that the lot levy will exceed $30,000 and require approval from IPART is said\nto only complicate matters further in terms of delay and finalisation by 30 March 2026.","stage":"unassigned","stage_basis":"unassigned","page":17},{"label":"79","code":null,"number":"79","title":"","text":"The applicant believes that the Council’s decision not to tender the public submissions\nmade by landowners and stakeholders following notification suggests real community\nopposition to the draft Planning Proposal. And, the Council’s failure to disclose the\nCouncil officers’ assessment reports for the public submissions only adds to the\nuncertainty of the whole process (T127:13; Ex 1R11). The applicant is also highly\ncritical of Mr Matthew’s failure to disclose during joint conferencing his knowledge about\nproposed major changes to zoning and other controls affecting the applicant’s land.\nAlbeit, the applicant embraces Mr Matthews’ reservations about the finalisation date of\n30 March 2026 and invites me to do the same (T177:11).","stage":"unassigned","stage_basis":"unassigned","page":17},{"label":"80","code":null,"number":"80","title":"","text":"The applicant submits the Department’s letter of 13 August 2025 with the slide shows of\nmore recent iterations of the Department’s plans for the Site ILP at Tab 14 Ex 1R2 (Ex\n1R3 Tab 7) demonstrates that the terms of the instruments are now very different to the\noriginal proposal. The design principles forwarded on 2 October 2025 under cover of a\nletter described as “indicative only” and “not relied upon for final designs and approvals”\nare also said to be far from settled.","stage":"unassigned","stage_basis":"unassigned","page":17},{"label":"81","code":null,"number":"81","title":"","text":"The applicant invites me to accept Mr Wilkes evidence over that of Mr Matthews and Mr\nPritchard and find that the changes identified in the draft Precinct Plan are so extensive\nthat they require a redraft of the DCP. And, the caveats entrenched in the draft such as\n“indicative only”, “subject to change” and “not be relied on for final designs and\napprovals” only underline the fact that the draft instrument is far from “settled” and that\nthe changes are not details but changes to matters of substance (AWS at [68]).","stage":"unassigned","stage_basis":"unassigned","page":17},{"label":"82","code":null,"number":"82","title":"","text":"In support of its position, the applicant relies on the same cases as the Council to argue\nthat the draft plan, draft DCP and Contributions Plan are not certain in their terms,\nincluding in relation to ‘substance’ nor “imminent in their making”: Architects Haywood\nand Bakker Pty Ltd v North Sydney Council [2000] NSWLEC 138 (Architects Haywood)\n(AWS at [59]-[60]). It submits, as the Department’s letter of 5 December 2024 identifies,\nthe Department intends to collaborate with the Council throughout the finalisation\nprocess of the rezoning process and there will be inevitable delays as public\nparticipation is enshrined in the EPA Act and is an object of the planning system in\nNSW.","stage":"unassigned","stage_basis":"unassigned","page":18},{"label":"83","code":null,"number":"83","title":"","text":"It is further submitted, given the work still to be done in the post exhibition and\nfinalisation stage (AWS at [70]), and the time elapsed to date that gazettal of the\ninstant, by 30 March 2026, or the foreseeable futures is not achievable.","stage":"unassigned","stage_basis":"unassigned","page":18},{"label":"84","code":null,"number":"84","title":"For these reasons, the applicant submits that the Court cannot place determining","text":"weight on the draft LTCPP.","stage":"unassigned","stage_basis":"unassigned","page":18},{"label":"85","code":null,"number":"85","title":"","text":"The applicant submits that the lack of certainty in relation to the planning aims and\nobjectives that will apply to the Site are matters that prevent the Court from even asking\nthe question as to whether the Proposed Development is “antipathetic” to the draft\ninstrument: Topwei One Pty Limited v Camden Council [2025] NSWLEC 1296.","stage":"unassigned","stage_basis":"unassigned","page":18},{"label":"86","code":null,"number":"86","title":"","text":"However, in the event that the Court considers that the exhibited draft plan should be\nafforded some weight, the applicant submits that the Court should accept that the\nbroader objectives of the LTCPP will not be thwarted by an approval of the application\n(AWS at [72]-[76]). The applicant relies on the evidence of Mr Pritchard, the Council’s\nplanner, that the proposed development is consistent with the Department’s latest\nversion of draft maps (Ex 1R3 Tab 10) and that the development is consistent with\nzoning, height and density controls (T249:9-13). Consistent with the reasoning in\nTerrace Tower Holdings Pty Ltd v Sutherland Shire Council (2003) 129 LGERA 195;\n[2003] NSWCA 289 (Terrace Tower) at [6]; Architects Haywood, and Edward Listin\nProperties Pty Ltd v North Sydney Council (No 2) [2000] NSWLEC 181 Talbot J at [15],\nthe applicant submits that its Proposed Development is consistent with the planning\napproach in the Draft Planning Proposal at (AWS at [76(a)-(d)]).","stage":"unassigned","stage_basis":"unassigned","page":18},{"label":"87","code":null,"number":"87","title":"The applicant submits that the evidence demonstrates that the Proposed Development","text":"is consistent with the latest draft maps from the Department. Furthermore, nothing in\nthe LTCPP requiring the height of buildings to go to 65m or 95m with an FSR of 5:1.\nThese development standards are maximums (AWS [82]). The Objective (1)(c) of the\nproposed height of buildings standard was intended to “facilitate higher density\ndevelopment” but not mandate it (Ex 1R2 p 822). Therefore, the Proposed\nDevelopment does not offend these draft controls.","stage":"unassigned","stage_basis":"unassigned","page":18},{"label":"88","code":null,"number":"88","title":"","text":"The applicant rejects the Council planner’s comment at [139] of the Joint Report (Ex\n1R5) that if the Proposed Development were approved, “it would deprive Council of the\nfuture opportunity to acquire the land proposed for public open space or the land\nproposed for a public road and linear plaza on the Site, once rezoning is finalised” and\nargues that this comment is made without proper foundation. It submits that it is always\nopen to the Department or Council to negotiate with the applicant to achieve a modified\noutcome. It can also acquire the Site or at least those parts of it that are proposed to be\nused for recreation.","stage":"unassigned","stage_basis":"unassigned","page":19},{"label":"89","code":null,"number":"89","title":"","text":"For these reasons the applicant submits that the grant of development consent does\nnot frustrate the Council or the Department’s vision for the land.","stage":"unassigned","stage_basis":"unassigned","page":19},{"label":"90","code":null,"number":"90","title":"","text":"Additionally, the applicant submits that the Council’s use of the draft instrument in the\nLTCRPP and DCP is an indirect way of imposing more onerous standards than those\noutlined in the current DCP in respect of this application.","stage":"unassigned","stage_basis":"unassigned","page":19},{"label":"91","code":null,"number":"91","title":"","text":"The applicant refers the Court to s 4.15(3A)(a) of the EPA Act which provides that if a\nproposed development complies with standards fixed for the development in a DCP,\nthen the consent authority cannot require more onerous standards with respect to that\naspect of the development. It contends that by the Council inviting the Court to impose\nmore onerous standards by “moving the Court to give significant weight to the LTCRPP,\nincluding the draft Precinct Plan, the draft DCP and draft ILP” is contrary to s 4.15(3A)\n(a) (Ex 1R5 p 222). Ultimately, the applicant submits that s 4.15(3A)(a) operates so as\nto prevent the consent authority from reforming the Proposed Development on the\nbasis that it is inconsistent with the LTCRPP and/or the Draft Precinct Plan as it\nconcerns building footprint, envelope and massing if it satisfies the standards relating to\nthose matters in the current DCP (Ex 1R5 Appendix J p 222)","stage":"unassigned","stage_basis":"unassigned","page":19},{"label":"92","code":null,"number":"92","title":"","text":"As the proposal complies with the current DCP controls in relation to building footprint,\nenvelope and massing, the controls for those matters under exhibited draft instruments\nare more onerous. It is submitted that cannot be relied upon to refuse consent if the\ndraft Planning Proposal is determined to be certain and imminent.\nConsideration - the Planning Proposal","stage":"unassigned","stage_basis":"unassigned","page":19},{"label":"93","code":null,"number":"93","title":"","text":"It is clear from the evidence particularly of Mr Wilkes that the applicant has been in\ncontact with the Department about the changes to the draft Planning Proposal and the\nbroad changes affecting sites A and B (which it owns) well before the hearing date.\nAnd, in respect to imminence, the date of 30 March 2026 was also made known to the\napplicant well before the hearing.","stage":"unassigned","stage_basis":"unassigned","page":19},{"label":"94","code":null,"number":"94","title":"","text":"The terms of and the detail of those communications between the applicant and the\nDepartment are referenced in Ex 1R3 and the transcript.","stage":"unassigned","stage_basis":"unassigned","page":19},{"label":"95","code":null,"number":"95","title":"Relevantly these communications with the Department confirm that none of the","text":"changes listed in the email of 7 August 2025 between the Department and the\napplicant’s Director (and depicted on the Site A & B slides of 13 August 2025; Ex 1R3\npp 24-25) in any way concerns the planning approach to the Site apart from Item 4.\nThese communications identify the need for an open space corridor on Site A but\nindicate the final shape will be subject to design principles which were still being\nrefined.","stage":"unassigned","stage_basis":"unassigned","page":20},{"label":"96","code":null,"number":"96","title":"","text":"Accepting that those were the issues that the Department was considering for Site A as\nof 13 August 2025, and that the Design Principles were in fact drafted by 2 October\n2025 (Ex 1R3 Tab 7, p 27) it is difficult to accept the applicant’s submission that it only\nbecame aware of changes to its land on 10 October 2025.","stage":"unassigned","stage_basis":"unassigned","page":20},{"label":"97","code":null,"number":"97","title":"","text":"That said, the exhibited Planning Proposal is a draft instrument, the consideration of\nwhich is required by s 4.15(1)(a)(ii) EPA Act is engaged. The fact that the Department is\nconsidering amendments does not change the characterisation of that draft instrument.\nIt must be acknowledged that the Act contemplates changes to a draft instrument even\nafter exhibition (s 3.35). Further, the exhibited draft falls within the wide rubric of\nconsiderations that fall within the concept of the public interest: Architects Haywood at\n[81].","stage":"unassigned","stage_basis":"unassigned","page":20},{"label":"98","code":null,"number":"98","title":"","text":"As the Council submits there cannot be an expectation for the purpose of legislation\n(Part 3 of the EPA Act) that requires re-exhibition of a draft planning instrument\nfollowing each and every alteration made to the draft in response to a consideration of\npublic submissions: Friends of Turramurra Inc v Minister for Planning [2011] NSWLEC\n128 at [158].","stage":"unassigned","stage_basis":"unassigned","page":20},{"label":"99","code":null,"number":"99","title":"","text":"The requirements of certainty and imminence are conjunctive.","stage":"unassigned","stage_basis":"unassigned","page":20},{"label":"100","code":null,"number":"100","title":"The critical question, addressing certainty, is whether the planning approach has been","text":"settled and any future amendments (at this time perhaps unknown) will be matters of\ndetail rather than substance. The imminence issue is then addressed by asking “…will\nthey be brought into force within the reasonably foreseeable future?”: Architects\nHaywood at [33].","stage":"unassigned","stage_basis":"unassigned","page":20},{"label":"101","code":null,"number":"101","title":"","text":"For the reasons identified by the Council (CWS at [79]-[90]), I am satisfied that the\nsubstance of the exhibited Planning Proposal is settled and certain. There may be\nmatters of detail changed but the evidence suggests that the purpose of the planning\ninstrument will not change.","stage":"unassigned","stage_basis":"unassigned","page":20},{"label":"102","code":null,"number":"102","title":"","text":"In that regard, it is to be noted that the Site is located within the inner core of the\nproposed town centre under the Planning Proposal in a prominent and strategically\nimportant location. Under the Planning Proposal, it seems settled that there will be a\ncivic park on the Site which will “create [a] more legible and central heart for the town\ncentre and provide a location capable of accommodating larger community events” (Ex\n1R2, p 566). It will include an east-west linear plaza which will provide a “wide footpath\nto allow for public seating and nooks and opportunity for retail breakout whilst\nmaintaining clear movement paths” (Ex R2 Tab 7, 28-29). The central north-south plaza\non the Site remains an important feature and as recently as 2 October 2025 the\nDepartment advised the applicant of the design principles for that plaza and the Council\nreceived a copy of those design principles on 8 October 2025 (Ex 1R3 33).","stage":"unassigned","stage_basis":"unassigned","page":20},{"label":"103","code":null,"number":"103","title":"","text":"In forming that view I accept that there is no onus on the Council in this case to prove\nthe draft instrument is both certain and imminent. Nor does anything turn on the fact\nthat a representative of the Department was not called to give evidence about the\nstatus of the draft instrument (a course that was also open to the applicant but not\ntaken) (CWS at [77]). I also do not read anything negative into the fact that the Court\ndid not receive copies of all submissions made as a result of the exhibition of the\nPlanning Proposal. It is not necessary in this case to know the precise nature of the\nissues raised by other submitters. The most relevant matter at this point is the attitude\nof the Department who relevantly have spent a 12-month period considering these\nsubmissions, as Mr Matthews explained to the Court (T158:16-37).","stage":"unassigned","stage_basis":"unassigned","page":21},{"label":"104","code":null,"number":"104","title":"As the Council identified, the proposed instrument has had four critical features","text":"affecting the site which have remained consistent throughout the entire process\nnamely:\n(1) north-south park/plaza;\n(2) east-west linear plaza;\n(3) greater height and density on the Site given its proximity directly adjacent to the\ntrain station; and\n(4) an east-west road partially located in the south-west corner of the Site.","stage":"unassigned","stage_basis":"unassigned","page":21},{"label":"105","code":null,"number":"105","title":"","text":"Whilst the north-south park appears to have narrowed into a 35m wide open space\npark/plaza, its location in the centre of the Site remains the same.","stage":"unassigned","stage_basis":"unassigned","page":21},{"label":"106","code":null,"number":"106","title":"","text":"There has been no change to the location of the east-west linear plaza since public\nexhibition.","stage":"unassigned","stage_basis":"unassigned","page":21},{"label":"107","code":null,"number":"107","title":"","text":"The change in the mechanism for the provision of those areas of open space is of no\nmoment. Contrary to AWS at [57(k)], the mechanism is not only the proposed DCP but\nprovisions in the proposed SEPP itself, as Mr Matthews explained (T162:1-8,45-50;\nT163:9-13), and as is clear from the Department’s September letter (Ex 1R3, 36). It is\nthe planning approach that those mechanisms embody which is critical. Whether it be\nby Council’s mechanism or the Department’s, the planning vision and approach is the\nsame; an area of open space central to the Site, intersected by an east-west linear\nplaza. In any event, as noted above, the view of the Department is that it is “unlikely”\nthat any developer “would choose to forego the incentives and not deliver any of the\nCivic Plaza” (Ex 1R11, 3).","stage":"unassigned","stage_basis":"unassigned","page":21},{"label":"108","code":null,"number":"108","title":"","text":"Similarly, whilst there have been changes to proposed density and height controls, the\nmaximums have increased. As the Council submits the Department has not only\nreinforced the planning approach of increased density in the inner core but “has\ndecided to double down” (CWS at [87]). That is, in its own words, partly a response to\nthe need for greater housing arising from the housing crisis (Ex 1R3, 36).","stage":"unassigned","stage_basis":"unassigned","page":22},{"label":"109","code":null,"number":"109","title":"The Applicant places heavy reliance on the references in the Departmental","text":"communications where it is said that “proposed changes are continuing to be refined\nand are subject to change” (Ex 1R3, 35, 37). Those references are hardly surprising as\nthe Department has not yet forwarded its changes to the Minister’s office (T165:17-21).\nHowever, the likelihood is that any further changes are likely to be mere matters of\ndetail, or if the Council’s suggestions regarding open space are accepted, the\napplicant’s Proposed Development will be even more inconsistent with the vision for the\nLeppington Town Centre.\nIs the Planning Proposal imminent?","stage":"unassigned","stage_basis":"unassigned","page":22},{"label":"110","code":null,"number":"110","title":"","text":"The word “imminence” means “temporal proximity, falling short of presence or arrival:\nTerrace Tower at [51]. However, it is a relative concept and depends upon context\n(CWS at [104]). In this case, the strategic process commenced in 2017 and on any\nview the process is well advanced. Considered in context, the making of the instrument\nis imminent.","stage":"unassigned","stage_basis":"unassigned","page":22},{"label":"111","code":null,"number":"111","title":"","text":"The evidence before the Court is that the Planning Proposal has been exhibited and is\nin the stage of finalisation by the Department (Ex 1R5, 265).","stage":"unassigned","stage_basis":"unassigned","page":22},{"label":"112","code":null,"number":"112","title":"Various sources confirm that the Department expects that the proposed instrument will","text":"be finalised by end of March 2026. Mr Wilkes confirmed that the Department had also\nindicated that date to him on 31 July 2025 when he was dealing with a DA for mixed\nuse development at 141 and 153 Byron Rd (T270:22-25; Ex 1R12, p 37).","stage":"unassigned","stage_basis":"unassigned","page":22},{"label":"113","code":null,"number":"113","title":"","text":"The Council was also advised in writing on 26 August 2025 by the solicitor of the\nDepartment of the anticipated finalisation date of March 2026. Mr Matthews gave\nevidence that as recently as 14 October 2025, officers of the Department had told him\nthat they remained “committed” to finalisation of the Planning Proposal by end of March\n2026, adding that it takes about 3 months within the Minister’s office (T227:5-130). The\nPlanning Proposal has been identified as State Significant and by all accounts the State\nGovernment is finalising the process after multiple years of planning at both the State\nand local levels as part of the redevelopment of western Sydney alongside the\nAerotropolis.","stage":"unassigned","stage_basis":"unassigned","page":22},{"label":"114","code":null,"number":"114","title":"","text":"While the precise finalised form and detail of the draft instrument and the documents\nunder it may change, the evidence is that the substance of the instrument is otherwise\ncertain: Architect Haywood at [33]; Terrace Tower at [47]; Village McEvoy Pty Limited v\nCouncil of the City of Sydney (No 2) 176 LGERA 119; [2010] NSWLEC 17 at [50]. As\nthe Council submits, the planning vision and approach is this same (above at 107).","stage":"unassigned","stage_basis":"unassigned","page":23},{"label":"115","code":null,"number":"115","title":"","text":"Further to my findings above, at [104]-[107], I do not accept, as the applicant contends,\nthat the references in the Department’s communications to “proposed changes [which]\nare continuing to be refined and are subject to change” to be anything other than\nmatters of detail.","stage":"unassigned","stage_basis":"unassigned","page":23},{"label":"116","code":null,"number":"116","title":"","text":"There is no evidence of the Department’s intention to re-exhibit its post exhibition\nchanges to the Council’s Planning Proposal. In the communications with the\nDepartment before the Court, the changes to the Planning Proposal are consistently\nreferred to as “post exhibition changes”. This is also consistent with communications to\nMr Matthews by Departmental officers at the fortnightly meetings (T219:36-40).","stage":"unassigned","stage_basis":"unassigned","page":23},{"label":"117","code":null,"number":"117","title":"","text":"Accordingly, I find that the Planning Proposal for the Leppington Town Centre is both\ncertain and imminent (Terrace Tower at [46]). The changes to the planning controls that\nrelate to the development Site in the Planning Proposal will be made, and made in the\nforeseeable future as the Council submitted. The Court has before it multiple\nreferences from multiple sources that the Department anticipates that the proposed\ninstrument will be finalised by the end of March 2026. The evidence supports a finding\nthat the finalisation of the draft is in the foreseeable future: Terrace Tower at [47].","stage":"unassigned","stage_basis":"unassigned","page":23},{"label":"118","code":null,"number":"118","title":"","text":"The evidence is that the applicant was made aware of the changes to the proposed\nzoning, height and FSR controls for the Site and its anticipated finalisation date of the\nend of March 2026 by the Department in July and August 2025 (T270:22-25).","stage":"unassigned","stage_basis":"unassigned","page":23},{"label":"119","code":null,"number":"119","title":"","text":"The mere fact that evidence is hearsay is not on its own sufficient to discount its\nreliability. Mr Matthews gave clear evidence of what he was told by the Department.\nThere is nothing to suggest that he was mistaken in his evidence, particularly given the\nconsistent position expressed by the Department in its other dealings (including with\nthe applicant). In that circumstance, to reject Mr Matthews’ evidence of what he was\ntold by the Department would require an adverse credibility finding which is simply not\navailable. Nor was any basis for such a finding put to him in cross-examination, as in\nfairness to Mr Matthews it should have been: Browne v Dunn (1893) 6 R 67 (HL).","stage":"unassigned","stage_basis":"unassigned","page":23},{"label":"120","code":null,"number":"120","title":"","text":"Mr Matthews was challenged in cross-examination about the likelihood of the 30 March\n2026 date being achieved, principally on the basis of four matters:\n(1) The Council officers’ response to the Department dated 30 September 2025\nproposed a re-exhibition of the proposed post-exhibition changes (on the basis\nthat there was a proposed significant increase in the open space adjacent to the\nexisting and proposed school sites);\n(2) The Council also requested that the proposed post-exhibition ILP should be\nsupported by an infrastructure delivery plan and a contributions plan which\nrelates to the amended ILP;\n(3) The contributions plan will need to be referred to IPART; and\n(4) The post-exhibition changes to the planning instrument are yet to be\ndocumented.","stage":"unassigned","stage_basis":"unassigned","page":23},{"label":"121","code":null,"number":"121","title":"","text":"Mr Matthews accepted that it would be “a difficult job to achieve” the 30 March 2026\ndate if re-exhibition was required and the amended infrastructure delivery plan and\ncontributions plan were required to be re-exhibited together with the proposed changes\nto the draft SEPP. However, as the Council submits the “if” is not made out.","stage":"unassigned","stage_basis":"unassigned","page":24},{"label":"122","code":null,"number":"122","title":"In re-examination, Mr Matthews confirmed that the Department’s position was that no","text":"further exhibition was required, and that the Department was not contemplating\ndelaying the making of the amendments to the SEPP until after changes were made to\nthe infrastructure delivery plan, contributions plan or draft DCP.","stage":"unassigned","stage_basis":"unassigned","page":24},{"label":"123","code":null,"number":"123","title":"","text":"I accept Mr Matthews evidence that the Planning Proposal is not following the Policy\nroute, but the Department’s streamlined process. The Minister is not obliged to re-\nexhibit the prosed instrument (or exhibit at all) (s 3.30(1) of the EPA Act) and the\nevidence suggests there will not be a renotification. I also accept that the finalisation of\nthe proposed instrument is not dependent upon the finalisation of the contributions\nplan. Nor do I have any understanding or need to understand the Department’s position\nin respect to the role of IPART of if it is intended that the contributions exceed $30,000.","stage":"unassigned","stage_basis":"unassigned","page":24},{"label":"124","code":null,"number":"124","title":"","text":"As the Court held in Architects Haywood at [33] and Terrace Tower at [47], the question\nis whether the planning approach which the new instruments adopt can be regarded as\ncertain to be brought into force within the reasonably foreseeable future. For the\nreasons stated the answer to that question is yes. Accordingly, I find the date for\nfinalisation of the Planning Proposal is in the foreseeable future and imminent, if not 30\nMarch 2026.\nWhether the Proposed Development is antipathetic to the settled Planning Proposal","stage":"unassigned","stage_basis":"unassigned","page":24},{"label":"125","code":null,"number":"125","title":"","text":"In answering this question, I accept that the focus of my inquiry is on the Site and not\nthe other sites impacted by the Planning Proposal, as the applicant sought to argue.","stage":"unassigned","stage_basis":"unassigned","page":24},{"label":"126","code":null,"number":"126","title":"","text":"The Site is at the “very heart of what is intended to become an important “transit\noriented” hub in western Sydney” (CWS at [115]).","stage":"unassigned","stage_basis":"unassigned","page":24},{"label":"127","code":null,"number":"127","title":"The conflict between the Planning Proposal and the Proposed Development is evident","text":"from the comparative table below:\nCurrent Council’s\nPlanning\nProposal\nState\nAmendments\nThe Development\nApplication\nZoning B3\nCommercial\nCore\nB4 Mixed Use\nExcept for\ncentral open\nspace: RE1\nPublic\nRecreation;\nMU1 Mixed UseN/A\nNorth-\nsouth\nPark\nNo park A public park,\nforming a town\nsquare,\nrunning north\nsouth in the\ncentre of the\nSite.\n35m wide public\n“park / plaza”\nrunning north-\nsouth in the\ncentre of the site.\nNo north-south park\nor plaza.\nShopping centre\nproposed in footprint,\nremoving planned\npublic open space\nand interrupting the\ndesired connectivity\nand view corridors to\nthe station.\nEast-\nWest\nLinear\nPlaza\nNo plaza Linear plaza\nrunning east-\nwest south of\nthe proposed\nroad.\nEast-west linear\nplaza retained\nNo east-west linear\nplaza.\nSubstantial buildings\nproposed in footprint\nof plaza, interrupting\nthe desired\nconnectivity and view\ncorridors.\nHeight\n(max)\n24m 65m 96m Max 24m (Shopping\ncentre)\nNot in keeping with\nthe envisioned\npyramid of heights\nand densities with\nthe greater focussed\nin the inner core area\naround the train\nstation.\nFSR\n(max)\nN/A 4.5:1 5:1 Shopping Centre:\n2.14:1 Hotel: 2.6:1\nNot in keeping with\nthe envisioned\npyramid of densities\nwith the greater\nfocused in the inner\ncore area around the\ntrain station.","stage":"unassigned","stage_basis":"unassigned","page":24},{"label":"128","code":null,"number":"128","title":"","text":"As state, the north-south park appears to have been narrowed into 35m wide open\nspace park/plaza, its location is settled as it remains in the centre of the Site. As such, it\naligns with the railway station and as the Department identified it is unlikely to change\ngiven the developer’s incentives to deliver the civic park (Ex 1R11, 3). The\nDepartment’s Design Principles for the Planning Proposal also emphasise the\nimportance of the continuous view corridor to the station being maintained and being\nreinforced as a primary “orientation element” (Ex 1R3, 27). Relevantly, there has been\nno change to the east-west plaza following public exhibition of the Planning Proposal.\nAs the Council submits these are critical elements of the planning approach are still\nreinforced in both the proposed DCP and the SEPP WPC as Mr Mathews explained in\nhis evidence (T162:1-8,45-50; T163:9-13).","stage":"unassigned","stage_basis":"unassigned","page":26},{"label":"129","code":null,"number":"129","title":"","text":"However, the evidence of both planners is that the current controls and planning\nproposal are based on “fundamentally different visions for Leppington” (Ex 1R5 113).","stage":"unassigned","stage_basis":"unassigned","page":26},{"label":"130","code":null,"number":"130","title":"Relevantly, Mr Smith, the applicant’s urban planning expert, conceded, at least in","text":"respect to the initial planning proposal, that:\n“It is an impossible task for a development application to comply with controls in forceand draft controls where - they are based on an entirely different precinct structure formatters where there is a clear conflict” (Ex 1R5 at 145).","stage":"unassigned","stage_basis":"unassigned","page":26},{"label":"131","code":null,"number":"131","title":"","text":"I accept Mr Smith’s expert assessment as it applies to this case. The Proposed\nDevelopment clearly conflicts with numerous components and policies advanced in the\nPlanning Proposal, as amended by the Department. I accept that “construction of an\nenclosed shopping centre would prevent the delivery of the planned plazas. The hotel\nwould do the same for the linear east -west plaza. It would remove the green entry\nstatement for those arriving at the Leppington town centre and funnel them into a\nprivate shopping centre” (CWS at [115]-[117]).","stage":"unassigned","stage_basis":"unassigned","page":26},{"label":"132","code":null,"number":"132","title":"","text":"As Mr Matthews identifies in the joint report the proposed civic park is critical to defining\nthe heart of the town centre. This is a key element of the draft precinct plan and draft\nDCP. Removal of the linear plaza significantly reduces the activation of the precinct as\na whole and reduces desired line though the Site (Ex 1R5 at 126). The purpose of the\npark is to deliver a significant public open space to support the planned commercial\nactivity, public transport users, workers in the proposed strategic centre and residents\nof the planned high density residential dwellings. The DA would thwart this by\npreventing future development of the Civic plaza (Ex 1R5 at 129(a)).","stage":"unassigned","stage_basis":"unassigned","page":26},{"label":"133","code":null,"number":"133","title":"","text":"As the Council submits (CWS at [130.4]) the DA would prevent the future development\nof the linear plaza which:\n(1) Has a purpose to enhance pedestrian amenity along Byron Road creating a\ncalmer, cooler and more present streetscape that supports ground floor\ncommercial uses and encourages activity and encourages views to Scalabrini\nCreek (Ex 1R5 at [130.4(i)]); and\n(2) Utilises pedestrian desire lines toward Leppington Station and between riparian\ncorridors, facilitating movement throughout what is envisaged to be some of the\nmost heavily trafficked parts of Leppington Town Centre and linking green\nspaces into a network (Ex 1R5 at [130.4(ii)]).","stage":"unassigned","stage_basis":"unassigned","page":27},{"label":"134","code":null,"number":"134","title":"By preventing these two elements the development “would completely break the link","text":"between the public domain green network focussed on the existing natural creek lines\nand its connection with the Town Centre Core and the Railway Station. It would remove\na significant street (the planned linear plaza), frustrating the delivery of the tree canopy\nand cool outdoor spaces” (Ex 1R5 at [130.5]).","stage":"unassigned","stage_basis":"unassigned","page":27},{"label":"135","code":null,"number":"135","title":"","text":"Mr Wilkes ultimately accepted that the Proposed Development does not allow for the\nnorth south plaza (T145:31); cannot accommodate the linear plaza in the area of the\nproposed shopping centre (T150:47) and is fundamentally inconsistent with the density\npyramid approach adopted by the Department (T141:45).Mr Wilkes also accepted that\nthe 25m east-west connecting road which is slated for acquisition under the\nDepartment’s ILP (presumably by the Council) would not be able to be provided if the\nDA were approved and proposed development constructed ( T150:42-49; T151:1).","stage":"unassigned","stage_basis":"unassigned","page":27},{"label":"136","code":null,"number":"136","title":"","text":"I do not accept Mr Smith and Mr Wilkes’ evidence that the planning vision in the\nProposed instrument is only “partly thwarted by the Proposed Development” because\nthe Site is only a small part of the land affected by the Planning Proposal. Their\nevidence seriously downplays the significance of the location of the Site and the\nplanning approach under the Planning Proposal. I prefer Mr Matthew’s evidence as it\nbetter reflects the planning purpose disclosed in the Planning Proposal (its underlying\ndocuments) and the approach taken by the Department in relation to the Site. And, I\naccept his expert assessment that the development is antipathetic to the vision,\nobjectives and intended outcomes of the planning proposal (Ex 1R5, 150).","stage":"unassigned","stage_basis":"unassigned","page":27},{"label":"137","code":null,"number":"137","title":"","text":"Moreover, I reject the applicant’s criticism of Mr Matthew for not disclosing in the joint\nreport his knowledge that the final form and size of the north-south plaza on the Site\nhad not been finalised (Ex 1R5, 149). I accept his apparent conflict between his\nobligations as an expert and his legal obligation not to commit a breach of confidence\nwith the Department (CWS at [134]-[135]). As it happened, his evidence was given on\nthe basis that the specific terms were not “set in stone”. As such, this does not warrant\ngiving less weight to his evidence, particularly in circumstances where I accept it largely\nreflects what is obvious from a review of the Planning Proposal and a comparison\nbetween the proposed development and the department maps as the Council\ncontends. That said, criticism can equally be made of Mr Wilkes’ failure to disclose in\nthe joint report his knowledge of changes to the proposed zoning, height and FSR and\nopen space following earlier meetings with officers of the Department (T270:22-25).","stage":"unassigned","stage_basis":"unassigned","page":27},{"label":"138","code":null,"number":"138","title":"","text":"For the reasons stated, as submitted by the Council (CWS at [114]-[137]), I find that the\nproposed development is antipathetic to the planning purpose of the draft Planning\nProposal. It substantially undermines the achievement of critical objectives that\nunderpin the proposed instrument and for that reason the DA should also be refused.\nThe section 4.15(3A) argument","stage":"unassigned","stage_basis":"unassigned","page":28},{"label":"139","code":null,"number":"139","title":"","text":"The applicant submitted that because the DA is fully compliant with the relevant\ncontrols in the current DCP that by reason of s 4.15(3A) of the EPA Act, the current\nDCP will override the Planning Proposal, and the Court will not be able to refuse the DA\nbased on the new requirements in the Planning Proposal (AWS at [86-131]).","stage":"unassigned","stage_basis":"unassigned","page":28},{"label":"140","code":null,"number":"140","title":"","text":"It contends that the Council’s use of the draft instruments in the LTCRPP and the DCP\nis an indirect way of imposing more onerous standards than those outlined in the\ncurrent DCP on the applicant (AWS at [88]; Letter of Advice dated 4 May 2025; Ex 1R5,\np 222).","stage":"unassigned","stage_basis":"unassigned","page":28},{"label":"141","code":null,"number":"141","title":"","text":"However, I do not accept their argument. A refusal of the application on the basis that it\nwill undermine the strategic planning purpose and vision contained in the proposed\ninstrument is not requiring a more onerous standard. It is a consideration of a draft\ninstrument under s 4.15(1)(a)(ii). Moreover, being compliant with a DCP does not mean\nthat a DA cannot be refused - the DCP is not alone determinative. The requirement in s\n4.15 is, as the Council submits, to consider the DCP and other matters - there is no\nhierarchy: Terrace Tower at [56].","stage":"unassigned","stage_basis":"unassigned","page":28},{"label":"142","code":null,"number":"142","title":"","text":"As the Council submits, it is almost inevitable that the new planning instrument will be\ninconsistent with the current controls. The weight to be given to the new instrument\ncannot depend on how closely the development aligns with existing controls that are\nabout to be replaced. If the extent of a development’s inconsistency with an existing\nDCP is a determinative matter under s 4.1(a)(ii), then the section would achieve\nnothing.","stage":"unassigned","stage_basis":"unassigned","page":28},{"label":"143","code":null,"number":"143","title":"","text":"The term “standards” for aspects of the development attracting the operation of s\n4.15(3A)(a) of the EPA Act is not defined other than to include performance criteria,\nhowever, it must be acknowledged that not all DCP provisions can be said to set\nstandards. Some provisions encourage a particular outcome. In all cases context is\nrelevant.","stage":"unassigned","stage_basis":"unassigned","page":29},{"label":"144","code":null,"number":"144","title":"","text":"The alleged standards that the applicant relies upon are set out at AWS at [104]-[109].\nNamely the land use “retail core” is to occupy the bulk of the Site, and buildings are to\nbe built to the boundary with zero setback and provide active street frontages.\nHowever, these are not standards with respect to an aspect of the development. The\nchanges proposed under the Planning Proposal are similar to requiring a complete\nredesign of the development on the Site. Contending that the DA should be refused on\nthe basis that the proposed development thwarts the objectives of the planning\nrationale of the Planning Proposal does not involve requiring more onerous standards.","stage":"unassigned","stage_basis":"unassigned","page":29},{"label":"145","code":null,"number":"145","title":"","text":"The meaning of “onerous” in s 4.15(3A) needs to be considered having regard to the\npurposes of the Act. Those purposes focus on environmental and planning matters and\nthe financial burden of compliance with the new LEP provisions as detailed in the\napplicant’s feasibility evidence, if relevant, is not established. There are no concept\nplans showing the various alternative developments such that the Court can assess\nfinancial feasibility. Focussing only on the draft DCP as the applicant identified in\nopening, I reject its argument in respect to s 4.15(3A) of the EPA Act for the same\nreasons (CWS at [140]-[157]).","stage":"unassigned","stage_basis":"unassigned","page":29},{"label":"146","code":null,"number":"146","title":"","text":"Finally, when I interrogate the task under s 4.15(1)(a)(ii), it requires that I take into\nconsideration “any proposed instrument that is or has been the subject of public\nconsultation under the Act and that has been notified to the consent authority” and\ndetermine the weight to be given to the draft instrument having regard to the certainty\nof its terms and the imminence of its making. In that consideration I will have regard to\nthe draft DCP as a component of the proposed instrument. However, I am not imposing\na more onerous standard than the current DCP. I am not imposing any standard.\nConclusion","stage":"unassigned","stage_basis":"unassigned","page":29},{"label":"147","code":null,"number":"147","title":"","text":"For the reasons stated development application no DA 2024/615/1 is refused consent.\nOrders","stage":"unassigned","stage_basis":"unassigned","page":29},{"label":"148","code":null,"number":"148","title":"","text":"The Court orders:\n(1) The appeal is dismissed.\n(2) Exhibits are returned.\nS Dixon\nSenior Commissioner of the Court\n**********\nDISCLAIMER - Every effort has been made to comply with suppression orders or statutory provisionsprohibiting publication that may apply to this judgment or decision. The onus remains on any personusing material in the judgment or decision to ensure that the intended use of that material does notbreach any such order or provision. Further enquiries may be directed to the Registry of the Court orTribunal in which it was generated.\nDecision last updated: 31 March 2026","stage":"unassigned","stage_basis":"unassigned","page":29}]});
